</h3><p>Joining two related tables on one key or a set of keys is a common operation. For example, the score sheets for the various subjects of a student&rsquo;s final exam can be joined on $\color{red}{\text{name}}$ and $\color{red}{\text{class}}$ into a single grade report; likewise, the various information tables of a company&rsquo;s employees can be joined and summarized by $\color{red}{\text{employee ID}}$. As these examples show, in a relational join the $\color{red}{\text{key}}$ is of central importance and is usually specified with the <code>on</code> parameter.</p>
        
